  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">REINTRODUCTION AND SURVEILLANCE OF THE SCARLET MACAW IN LOS TUXTLAS, VERACRUZ, AND THE CONSERVATION OF NATIVE SPECIES WITH COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION IV</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The scarlet macaw (Ara macao cyanoptera) is one of the most emblematic and representative species of the tropical rain forests of the Americas. Unfortunately, habitat loss and trapping of chicks for the domestic and international illegal trade are driving the species to the extinction. Since 2013, Bosque Antiguo, A.C. with support from the Mexico program has been working on the reintroduction of the scarlet macaw in Los Tuxtlas Biosphere Reserve (LTBR) with the goals of establishing a self-sustaining population of 500 scarlet macaws and restoring the LTBR forests lost to decades of deforestation and unsustainable use of its natural resources. Through this two-year grant Bosque Antiguo, A.C. will: 1) reintroduce the two new groups of young macaws raised in captivity for a total of 300 birds in the wild; 2) monitor the free scarlet macaw population in collaboration with the Scarlet Macaw Community Monitoring Network; 3) carry out nest monitoring activities and install at least 30 nest boxes annually in sites protected by the local communities; 4) guide community-led reforestation and restoration efforts along 100 hectares of degraded tropical rain forest to increase species habitat, foraging, nesting and roosting areas; 5) continue with the rehabilitation and reintroduction of wild parrots (blue-crowned parrot, yellow-headed parrot, and white-crowned parrot) rescued from the illegal pet trade; 6) provide technical assistance to local communities on sustainable production practices such as apiculture and production of marmalades as an alternative to cattle ranching and poaching of local fauna; and 7) implement a social marketing campaign for the members of the local communities, elementary schools, tourists and visitors using communication strategies customized to each audience.</narrative>
  </description>
